---
import AppShell from '../../layouts/AppShell.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';

export const prerender = true;

const title = 'AI Automation Services | Neil Busque, New Jersey';
const description = 'AI automation services for small teams. I map where your hours go, then build the agents and workflows that take that work off your desk. One person, start to finish, live in days.';
const canonical = 'https://busqueneil.com/services/ai-automation-services';

const service = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: 'AI Automation Services',
  url: canonical,
  image: 'https://busqueneil.com/og.png',
  provider: { '@id': 'https://busqueneil.com/#person' },
  areaServed: ['New Jersey', 'New York', 'New York City', 'United States', 'Remote'],
  serviceType: ['AI automation', 'Workflow automation', 'Business process automation', 'CRM automation', 'AI agent development'],
  description,
};
const faq = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What are AI automation services?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "AI automation services turn repetitive business work into systems that run on their own. A build usually combines plain workflow automation, which follows fixed rules, with AI agents that handle the steps needing judgment, such as reading a messy email, deciding whether a lead is worth calling, or drafting a reply. The result is that work still happens, but nobody on your team has to sit and do it."
        }
    },
    {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What can actually be automated?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Anything repetitive with a clear trigger. The most common builds are lead capture and routing, follow-up sequences across email and SMS, data entry and enrichment, CRM cleanup, recurring reports, and content or research drafting. If a person on your team does it the same way more than a few times a week, it is a candidate."
        }
    },
    {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How long does an AI automation build take?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A single workflow is usually days rather than months. Neil Busque shipped 40 plus web apps, agents, and automations in 2026, most within days of the idea. Scope drives the timeline more than complexity does, so one well-defined workflow moves fast."
        }
    },
    {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Do I work with an agency or with you directly?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Directly, every time. There is no account manager, no junior, and no handoff. The person on the first call is the person who builds it and the person you message when something needs changing."
        }
    },
    {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Which tools do you build on?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "n8n, Zapier, and GoHighLevel for workflow orchestration, and Claude with MCP tools for the agent layer, wired into whatever CRM, inbox, and database you already use. The stack follows the problem, not the other way around."
        }
    }
],
};
---
<AppShell title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} active="hire">
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <Breadcrumb items={[
      { name: 'Home', url: 'https://busqueneil.com/' },
      { name: 'Services', url: 'https://busqueneil.com/services' },
      { name: 'AI Automation Services', url: canonical },
    ]} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(service)} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faq)} />
  </Fragment>

  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="idx">&#8727;</span> Services &#183; AI Automation</p>
    <h1>AI automation <span class="accent">services</span>.</h1>
    <p class="lede">I am Neil Busque. I build AI automation for small teams in New Jersey, New York, and remote: the agents and workflows that take repetitive work off your desk and run it without you.</p>
  </header>

  <article class="article-page">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What AI automation services actually cover</h2>
      <p>Most teams do not need a platform. They need four or five specific jobs taken off their desk. These are the ones that come up most:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Leads.</strong> Capture, enrich, route, and follow up automatically, so nothing sits in an inbox going cold.</li>
        <li><strong>Follow-up and nurture.</strong> Email and SMS sequences triggered by what people actually do, not by a calendar.</li>
        <li><strong>Ops and admin.</strong> Data entry, enrichment, reporting, CRM hygiene, and the handoffs between tools that quietly eat hours.</li>
        <li><strong>Research and content.</strong> Agents that read, summarize, and draft on a schedule so a person only has to edit.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Plain automation or an AI agent</h2>
      <p>Not everything needs AI, and putting a model where a rule would do is how automation gets expensive and unpredictable. When the steps never change, I build a plain workflow. When the task needs judgment, reading messy input, deciding what matters, writing something a human will send, I bring in an agent. You get the right tool per job. <a href="/guides/build-ai-agent-claude-code">Here is how the agents are built.</a></p>

      <h2>How the work runs</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>We find the time sinks.</strong> One call, and I ask where the hours go. Usually the expensive work is not the work people complain about.</li>
        <li><strong>I scope it before I build.</strong> You see exactly what gets automated and what does not, so nothing about the finished build surprises you.</li>
        <li><strong>I build it.</strong> Days, not quarters, and you see progress as it goes.</li>
        <li><strong>You keep it or I keep running it.</strong> Your choice. It is your system either way.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Why one person instead of an agency</h2>
      <p>Automation breaks. A webhook changes, an API deprecates, a form adds a field. With an agency, that is a ticket, a queue, and a junior who did not build the thing. With me it is a message to the person who wrote it. That is the whole difference, and it is why I do not scale this by hiring.</p>

      <h2>Where I work</h2>
      <p>I am based in Elizabeth, New Jersey and work with teams across the NJ and NYC metro, plus remote clients across the United States. Most of the work is remote regardless of where you are, and I am reachable in Eastern time.</p>

      <h2>Proof, not promises</h2>
      <p>You can check the work first. In 2026 I shipped 40 plus web apps, PWAs, AI agents, and automations, and earlier shipped 20 plus production n8n workflows across 10 plus client accounts, including a one-click onboarding system that cut onboarding from 30 days to 7. You can browse <a href="/work">the work</a> and read <a href="/guides">the guides</a> before you decide anything.</p>

      <h2>Start here</h2>
      <p>Tell me where your team loses hours and I will tell you honestly whether automation is the right fix. <a href="/contact">Send me the problem</a>, or see <a href="/services/ai-automation-consultant">what an AI automation consultant does</a> and <a href="/services/ai-agent-development">how AI agent development works</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <div class="hire-cta v8-card">
    <span class="eyebrow">The guarantee</span>
    <p class="guarantee">I keep working until you are proud of it. If I am not the right fit, I will tell you on the first call.</p>
    <div class="hero-ctas">
      <a class="btn-sun" href="/contact">Tell me what you need</a>
      <a class="btn-ghost" href="https://wa.me/19083164140?text=Hi%20Neil%2C%20I%20want%20to%20talk%20about%20a%20build">Or message on WhatsApp</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</AppShell>
